
  [1mModule layout[0m (new directory, ES modules):

  validation/
    validators.js   built-in rule factories
    validate.js     the core: data + rules -> errors
    bindForm.js     the DOM binder (section 2)
    index.js        re-exports the public surface

  [1mValidator contract.[0m A validator is [38;5;153m(value, data) => string | null[39m — an error message, or [38;5;153mnull[39m if it passes. That
  signature is the whole extension point: a custom rule is a local function, no core change. Taking [38;5;153mdata[39m as the second
  argument means cross-field rules (confirm-password, end-date-after-start) work without a special mechanism.

  [1mCore function.[0m

  [33mvalidate[39m(data, rules) -> { [36mvalid[39m: boolean, [36merrors[39m: { [field]: message } }

  [38;5;153mrules[39m is [38;5;153m{ fieldName: [validator, ...] }[39m. Within a field, the first failing validator wins and the rest are skipped;
  across fields, every field is checked so the user sees all their problems at once. One message per field rather than
  an array — it matches how a form actually renders (one error line per input) and keeps callers from having to decide
  which of three messages to show. If you'd rather collect every failure per field, say so now; it's the return shape,
  so it's not free to change later.

  [1mBuilt-ins I'd ship:[0m [38;5;153mrequired()[39m, [38;5;153mminLength(n)[39m, [38;5;153mmaxLength(n)[39m, [38;5;153mpattern(regex, message)[39m. Each takes an optional trailing
  custom message. Deliberately [3mnot[0m shipping [38;5;153memail()[39m — [38;5;153mpattern[39m covers it in one line, and a shared half-correct email
  regex is worse than none. It's easy to add the first time a real form needs it.

  [1mOne wrinkle to settle.[0m ES modules mean Node needs to know these files aren't CommonJS. Cleanest is [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in
  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, which requires converting [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — 5 lines total, unrelated to this feature but
  otherwise left broken. The no-touch alternative is naming the new files [38;5;153m.mjs[39m, leaving [38;5;153msrc/[39m alone at the cost of a
  permanently mixed repo. I lean toward converting; it's smaller than it sounds and leaves one module system.

  Does that look right so far?

